WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.080
When I sit down and read
I'm, I'm gaining more words.

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:06.180
Hi, Steve Kaufmann here.

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:11.100
And, um, today I want to talk about
reading and language learning.

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:14.940
So if you enjoy these videos,
please subscribe and click on

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:16.530
the bell for notifications.

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:20.340
If you follow me on a podcast
service, please leave a review.

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:25.740
Now, for those of you who have been
following my channel, you know,

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:30.525
that I emphasize listening and
reading as being the most important

00:00:30.585 --> 00:00:33.615
activities for acquiring a language.

00:00:33.915 --> 00:00:36.705
Of course, we want to speak, we
eventually want to speak a lot.

00:00:36.885 --> 00:00:38.055
Our goal is to speak.

00:00:38.295 --> 00:00:42.705
We want to speak well, but the
emphasis should be on listening and

00:00:42.705 --> 00:00:47.114
reading because it's something we
can do whenever we want on our own.

00:00:47.295 --> 00:00:51.525
And it really does help our brain
become familiar with the language.

00:00:51.915 --> 00:00:56.144
Uh, if you want to read up on it you
should check out language acquisition

00:00:56.835 --> 00:01:03.165
with Stephen Krashen K R A S H E
N, who is in my view, the leading

00:01:03.375 --> 00:01:05.474
explainer of how we learn languages.

00:01:06.465 --> 00:01:12.825
So I did a video on listening in French
and it has been very well received.

00:01:12.854 --> 00:01:19.005
And so, uh, I'm going to do videos on the
subject of listening in other languages,

00:01:19.035 --> 00:01:20.775
maybe Spanish, Japanese, Chinese.

00:01:20.775 --> 00:01:22.275
Let me know what you would like to hear.

00:01:22.275 --> 00:01:26.005
Of course, I can't necessarily do
it all that well in the languages

00:01:26.024 --> 00:01:30.164
that I don't speak as well, but
today I want to focus on reading.

00:01:31.035 --> 00:01:33.735
Uh, you'll notice in behind
me is a new backdrop.

00:01:34.005 --> 00:01:35.054
It shows a bookshelf.

00:01:35.115 --> 00:01:36.645
So I have two backdrops.

00:01:37.065 --> 00:01:42.195
I have my wooden plank backdrop,
which is in behind, and I have my

00:01:42.225 --> 00:01:46.604
bookshelf backdrop because I love books.

00:01:46.664 --> 00:01:47.354
So I love reading.

00:01:47.354 --> 00:01:50.535
I think reading is a
phenomenal technology.

00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:56.670
Uh, if we think that that humans did
not have the ability to read for most

00:01:56.670 --> 00:02:02.160
of our existence, as, I mean, if you
go back before homosapiens, uh, I mean,

00:02:02.190 --> 00:02:06.900
for the amount of time that hominids
or whatever they're called have been on

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:10.770
this, uh, earth during the whole process
of evolution, there was no reading.

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:17.820
And so all of a sudden, as humankind
developed this technology of representing

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:25.800
words and thought through symbols, which
started however many years ago, and

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:28.590
I'm not going to go into the history
of writing, but there have been many

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:30.990
writing systems today in the world.

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:35.190
There are many writing systems,
all of a sudden humans, our brains

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:41.010
have to develop this ability to
convert symbols into meaning into

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:43.160
perhaps sound perhaps meaning.

00:02:44.049 --> 00:02:48.459
And, um, it's amazing that our brains
have become very good at doing this.

00:02:48.459 --> 00:02:51.940
It's not a simple task, I think,
although I'm not a, any kind of a

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:58.390
cognitive scientist, uh, I find that
if I want to, you know, learn how to

00:02:58.390 --> 00:03:02.470
do something or learn about anything,
reading is the fastest way to get there.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:07.590
Uh, listening is great because I
can do that while doing other tasks.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:12.060
Reading I have to basically commit
myself to reading this thing, but with

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:17.790
reading, I can take notes and reading
is, it's this wonderful sense that, that

00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:23.160
these symbols are being converted into
meaning and are somehow miraculously by

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:28.500
our brains using sort of a capability
that for most of our history, as,

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:31.950
as you know, beings, we didn't have.

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:39.570
And it's certainly true that, that
developing the ability to read well is one

00:03:39.570 --> 00:03:45.030
of the greatest sort of things that we can
do to ensure that we can be successful.

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:48.424
You know, academically,
professionally and so forth.

00:03:48.424 --> 00:03:50.295
Reading is tremendously powerful.

00:03:50.445 --> 00:03:53.385
That's not to say that you
can't learn through experience.

00:03:53.385 --> 00:03:57.734
I know many people who are not great
readers, but who, you know, develop

00:03:57.765 --> 00:04:02.445
through their experiences working with
machines or around any other kind of

00:04:02.445 --> 00:04:06.945
thing where they accumulate experience
through watching other people do things.

00:04:06.945 --> 00:04:10.185
And then they do them themselves and
they develop a tremendous ability,

00:04:10.185 --> 00:04:11.714
which makes them very successful humans.

00:04:12.644 --> 00:04:14.234
But reading is a shortcut.

00:04:14.445 --> 00:04:18.855
Reading is tremendously powerful and
there's all kinds of statistics that

00:04:18.855 --> 00:04:26.474
show that how well we read is a bigger
indicator of let's call it economic,

00:04:26.474 --> 00:04:31.094
social success, professional success,
even than the number of years spent,

00:04:31.815 --> 00:04:33.765
uh, in school or at universities.

00:04:33.825 --> 00:04:34.815
So reading is powerful.

00:04:34.995 --> 00:04:36.885
The same is true for language learning.

00:04:38.085 --> 00:04:38.875
I like listening.

00:04:39.255 --> 00:04:40.575
It's so easy to do.

00:04:41.055 --> 00:04:45.344
I spend more time listening than
anything else because it's so easy to do.

00:04:45.825 --> 00:04:52.065
Um, but I realized when I sit down
and read I'm, I'm gaining more

00:04:52.065 --> 00:04:59.414
words like vocabulary acquisition is
easier through reading than through

00:04:59.414 --> 00:05:00.925
listening, at least in my experience.

00:05:01.125 --> 00:05:06.735
Because in a way, the sort of visual
representation of a word is, is the

00:05:06.735 --> 00:05:11.474
written form of that word to me, rather
than whatever that word represents.

00:05:11.474 --> 00:05:16.185
So it would be, if it were English,
R E D rather than the color red.

00:05:16.395 --> 00:05:21.960
So the written word is a powerful
representative of the word and if

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:25.860
I have that in my brain, I find
it easier to remember vocabulary.

00:05:26.130 --> 00:05:30.990
That's part of what makes learning Persian
and Arabic more difficult because I'm

00:05:30.990 --> 00:05:36.900
not yet so comfortable with that script
that I can automatically call up the sort

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:39.750
of written representation of the word.

00:05:40.830 --> 00:05:44.250
But so reading, I, and I, I say to
myself, I want to do more reading

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:49.680
and as with all of our activities
in languages, variety is good.

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:53.190
So I read on my iPad using LingQ there.

00:05:53.190 --> 00:05:54.480
I can look words up.

00:05:54.780 --> 00:05:58.980
I can listen in the case of Persian
and Arabic, where the pronunciation

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:03.900
is not always obvious, which is also
obviously the case in English that

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:08.280
the pronunciation is not obvious, text
to speech, and I can hear the word

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:09.960
pronounced and that'll help me read it.

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:13.980
So there's a number of reasons why
reading on the iPad, reading in LingQ,

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:19.185
saving words  and phrases is an easier
way for me to read, but at the same time,

00:06:19.215 --> 00:06:21.435
I think it's good to read paper books.

00:06:22.094 --> 00:06:26.505
And when I was learning, say Slavic
languages and even say Polish or

00:06:26.505 --> 00:06:30.885
Czech, I would enjoy reading paper
books, even Romanian, where my

00:06:30.885 --> 00:06:35.195
vocabulary was more limited because
I was so familiar with the alphabet.

00:06:35.635 --> 00:06:39.030
It's more difficult for me to
do that in Persian and Arabic.

00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:42.210
So I'm still at the stage
where I need, I'm still riding

00:06:42.210 --> 00:06:43.830
a tricycle, training wheels.

00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:49.020
I'm still at the stage where I need
the assist of LingQ, looking up words,

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:53.220
hearing the word pronounced this kind
of thing in order to enjoy reading.

00:06:53.250 --> 00:06:59.640
But still I do find that, whereas
I would, you know, tend to say,

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:06.015
listen to a podcast, you know, From
Al-Jazeera now that more and more

00:07:06.045 --> 00:07:11.625
content is being, uh, you know, pushed
at us on LingQ in terms of things

00:07:11.625 --> 00:07:13.875
that we can import for our own use.

00:07:14.354 --> 00:07:17.085
And a lot of that is
say newspaper articles.

00:07:17.115 --> 00:07:21.615
I find it quicker to just take that
newspaper article and read it in

00:07:21.615 --> 00:07:26.855
LingQ rather than going through the
process of grabbing a YouTube video,

00:07:26.855 --> 00:07:31.275
taking the URL and getting the MP3
file and getting it transcribed.

00:07:31.605 --> 00:07:34.815
And so forth and so on, where
listening is easy and it's powerful,

00:07:34.845 --> 00:07:38.925
powerful for getting us sort of
momentum in the language for training

00:07:38.925 --> 00:07:40.995
us towards speaking the language.

00:07:41.025 --> 00:07:45.405
I associate listening with speaking and
reading with vocabulary acquisition.

00:07:45.565 --> 00:07:48.885
Of course, both are involved in both.

00:07:48.914 --> 00:07:51.375
So reading helps you speak
because you're acquiring more

00:07:51.375 --> 00:07:53.804
words, listening helps you read.

00:07:54.015 --> 00:07:59.234
But if I were to say, what is the
sort of emphasis in listening?

00:07:59.234 --> 00:07:59.835
It's speaking.

00:08:00.044 --> 00:08:01.604
What is the emphasis in reading?

00:08:01.664 --> 00:08:03.405
It's vocabulary acquisition.

00:08:03.765 --> 00:08:08.474
And so I just wanted to, and I will
continue making videos about listening and

00:08:08.474 --> 00:08:13.094
the importance of listening, but I don't,
I don't want to deemphasize reading and,

00:08:13.094 --> 00:08:16.620
uh, while reading is not something you
can do while you're doing other tasks,

00:08:16.650 --> 00:08:21.510
like is the case with listening, it's
something that's relatively portable and

00:08:21.510 --> 00:08:25.440
you can do it when you have 15 minutes or
10 minutes, or you're waiting somewhere.

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:31.140
And so you can read either because you
have a book paper book, or because you

00:08:31.140 --> 00:08:35.310
have your iPhone and you can just start
reading say in the language that you're

00:08:35.310 --> 00:08:39.720
learning, whether on LingQ or some
other, uh, website that you're following.

00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:41.039
So reading.

00:08:41.070 --> 00:08:41.159
Okay.

00:08:41.880 --> 00:08:44.410
Extremely important in
language acquisition.

00:08:44.970 --> 00:08:49.950
And, um, but with that, I will
try to do more videos in different

00:08:49.950 --> 00:08:51.570
languages about the power of listening.

00:08:51.810 --> 00:08:54.990
And if you have some requests
there, please let me know.

00:08:55.260 --> 00:08:56.160
Thank you for listening.

00:08:56.520 --> 00:08:57.160
Thank you for listening.

